%%WIKI-SYNC-META%%
title: b1 Kinnect longitudinal two-site study
syntax: xwiki/2.1
%%WIKI-SYNC-META%%
**Abstract.** Study design for the Summative Test of Kinnect, the System of the Meaningful Connections concept. A controlled trial at two care homes over a five-week window: one home runs Kinnect (one-to-one conversations, compatibility matching and icebreakers), the other keeps its standard activity programme. It tests whether Kinnect increases peer connections and time in communal spaces and reduces time in the bedroom (primary Claim), and whether first-time users with cognitive impairment find it easy to understand (secondary Claim). Designed, not run: no results and no Evidence yet.

= Evaluation Report [ER-03] =

== Shared fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|ER-03
|Name|Longitudinal two-site controlled study of Kinnect (study design)
|Status|In Progress (designed, not run)
|Mode|Summative
|Artifact used|[[AT-01 Kinnect social connection-maker>>doc:3 Evaluation.a Artifacts.a1 Kinnect.WebHome]] — specifically **Build 3 (Summative Pepper+ / Pepper Enhanced Build)**. Whereas **Build 1 (##sce-poc##)** is implemented as a browser-based Pepper simulation with a motion-tracked chest-tablet GUI overlay for formative evaluation, this summative study design assumes **Build 3** on **Pepper+ (Pepper Enhanced)**: full-duplex simultaneous speaking and listening plus an on-device hardware accelerator (TECH-18) running a local LLM so Pepper can hold one-to-one conversations, build persona profiles, compute compatibility scores and offer icebreakers locally, as described in //Intervention: matching and facilitation// below.
|Evaluation Method(s) applied|[[EM-08 Two-site controlled trial>>doc:1 Foundation.b5 Evaluation Methods.b5\.1 Prototype, observation and two-home comparison.WebHome]], addressing Claims only. EM-08 is the summative counterpart of the formative two-home comparison EM-03.
|Ethics|(((
**Review.** The protocol is submitted to the Human Research Ethics Committee (HREC). Recruitment starts only after institutional ethical clearance.

**Consent.** Written informed consent from each resident's legal representative or guardian, together with ongoing verbal and behavioural assent from the resident (Black et al., 2010; Slaughter et al., 2007). All robot sessions and peer introductions are voluntary. Verbal or non-verbal dissent, such as turning away or declining an invitation, is respected immediately (Black et al., 2010; Slaughter et al., 2007).

**Protections.** Staff and researchers pause at once if a participant shows signs of agitation, confusion or frustration during a robot or peer interaction. Agitation is tracked throughout with CMAI criteria (Black et al., 2010; Cohen-Mansfield et al., 1989). Badges and the bedroom beacon are used only for residents who give separate consent for them. Speech is processed on the robot and no audio or transcript is kept. Personal data are handled as set out in the //Data management plan// below.
)))
|Introduction|(((
**Claims addressed.** The study design states two hypotheses and calls them premises. In SCE terms both are Claims, because they are about effects on people. UC01 to UC03 and their Claims now exist, but neither study hypothesis maps exactly to one current Claim ID (see //Open items//). UC02-F3-CL1 is the closest existing Claim to the primary hypothesis.

* **Primary Claim.** Kinnect increases peer connections among residents with early- to middle-stage dementia, measured as dyadic social interactions and time spent in communal spaces, and reduces bedroom isolation.
* **Secondary Claim.** Kinnect is intuitive and easy to understand for first-time users living with cognitive impairment.

**Background.** Residents of assisted living and dementia care homes are often socially isolated, and spend large parts of the day alone in their private bedrooms, away from communal areas (Jansen et al., 2017). Cognitive decline can make it harder to start a conversation with a peer spontaneously. Social network theory finds that ties form faster and last longer between people who share life histories, interests and ways of talking, a principle known as homophily (McPherson et al., 2001). Structured reminiscence about nostalgic eras, music and life histories has been associated with improved mood and communication in dementia care (Woods et al., 2018).

Kinnect is an autonomous conversational companion robot designed to act as a social catalyst. It does not replace human contact. In one-to-one, person-centred conversations it learns each resident's background, hobbies and conversational pace. It then computes compatibility between residents and brings matched residents together through tailored conversational icebreakers (Wang et al., 2026).

**Why a Summative Test.** Both Claims are about what happens to residents of a real care home over several weeks: whether they meet peers, talk with them and spend less time in their rooms. Only a study in that setting can support, qualify or challenge them.
)))
|Method|(((
**Participants.** Residents with early- to medium-stage dementia or Mild Cognitive Impairment (MCI) who live in residential care, at two similarly equipped care homes. Care staff at both homes take part through training, protocol support and the debriefing interviews. Target: 30 residents recruited per home, 60 in total, so that 25 per home complete (see //Sample size// under //Hypotheses and analysis plan//).

Inclusion:

* A formal diagnosis of early- to medium-stage dementia or MCI, operationalised as a Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE) score of 14 to 24 (Folstein et al., 1975) or a Montreal Cognitive Assessment (MoCA) score of 10 to 22 (Nasreddine et al., 2005).
* Able to move to or spend time in communal areas, on their own or with standard assistive aids or staff support.
* Able to talk in the main language Kinnect supports.
* Written informed consent from a legal representative or guardian, with ongoing verbal and behavioural assent from the resident (Black et al., 2010; Slaughter et al., 2007).

Exclusion:

* Cognitive impairment severe enough to prevent basic verbal interaction or understanding of conversational prompts (Folstein et al., 1975).
* Severe sensory deficits, such as unaddressed profound hearing loss or severe visual impairment, that prevent audio-visual interaction with the robot.
* Acute medical conditions or clinical restrictions that rule out social contact with other residents.
* Severe behavioural and psychological symptoms of dementia, such as extreme baseline agitation or paranoia toward electronic or robotic hardware, assessed at baseline screening (Cohen-Mansfield et al., 1989).

**Experimental design.** Between-subject, longitudinal cluster-controlled trial (Donner & Klar, 2000): a one-month intervention within a five-week operational window, at two homes that run at the same time. One home is allocated to the intervention arm and the other to the control arm, which limits cross-exposure and contamination between the arms (Chenoweth et al., 2014; Donner & Klar, 2000).

* **Intervention arm.** Kinnect engages residents in person-centred dialogue and matches them with peers by compatibility (the "Vibing Algorithm"). Matched residents are offered voluntary shared time in the communal lounge (Wang et al., 2026; Woods et al., 2018).
* **Control arm.** The home's standard social activity programme: communal lounge time on one's own, or the home's usual group activities, without algorithmic matching and without robot icebreakers.

Independent variable: arm, nominal with two levels (intervention, control), allocated per home. Dependent variables: the Measures below.

**Tasks.** Intervention arm: one-to-one sessions with Kinnect in week 1, introductions to a matched peer in weeks 2 and 3, and joint icebreaker sessions with that peer in weeks 3 and 4. Every session and introduction is optional. Control arm: the usual daily routine and activities.

**Measures.** M-19 to M-23, defined on the §1.2 [[b4.1 Introductions, friendships and system checks>>doc:1 Foundation.b4 Measures.b4\.1 Introductions, friendships and system checks.WebHome]] page, with their scoring rules and proposed thresholds.

|=Domain|=Measure|=Source
|Well-being and affect|M-19 Agitation and observed affect|CMAI from primary caregivers (Cohen-Mansfield et al., 1989); OERS around sessions, as in M-03; staff log of agitation events
|Social connections and integration|M-20 Peer connections and communal time|Real-time location system (RTLS) proximity badges (Grigorovich et al., 2021; Jansen et al., 2017), checked against EM-02 room scans
|Conversational engagement|M-21 Conversational engagement|Speech recognition, transcript NLP and acoustic analysis on the robot (TECH-18), in one-to-one sessions and joint sessions (Fraser et al., 2016; Luz et al., 2021)
|Bedroom isolation time|M-22 Bedroom isolation time|Badge proximity to a fixed beacon in the resident's bedroom (Jansen et al., 2017)
|First-time usability (secondary Claim)|M-23 First-time usability|Week 1 interaction logs, response latency (Luz et al., 2021), an observational usability checklist and a first-impression question

**Procedure.** Five phases, from four weeks before the intervention to week 5. See //Study protocol// below.

**Material.** AT-01 Kinnect on Pepper with an on-device accelerator (TECH-18) at the intervention home. Proximity badges for consenting participants, with fixed anchors in common areas and beacons in bedrooms, at both homes. A tablet for EM-02 room scans. MMSE or MoCA and CMAI forms; OERS sheets; the staff agitation log; the M-23 usability checklist. Consent forms, including the separate consent for badges.
)))

